    [38;5;153mgetSession()[39m parses inside a try, validates that [38;5;153muserId[39m and [38;5;153musername[39m are non-empty strings, and on any failure
    clears the key and returns [38;5;153mnull[39m. A bad record never propagates as a half-populated session.
  - [1mQuota exceeded on write[0m — caught and logged; the in-memory value still updates, so the current page keeps working.

  Reads return [38;5;153mnull[39m for "not logged in" — never [38;5;153mundefined[39m, never a partial object. That's one shape for callers to
  check, which matters when the callers are forms nobody's written yet.

  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m itself has no failure path worth designing while it's a stub — it always resolves success. When the real [38;5;153mfetch
[39m  lands, rejection handling goes there, and the handler's [38;5;153mawait[39m already has the shape for it.

  [1mTesting.[0m This is where I need a decision rather than a confirmation. The repo has no tooling of any kind: no test
  runner, no lint, no format config, no [38;5;153mscripts[39m, no dependencies. The design above is deliberately testable — [38;5;153msession.js
[39m  and [38;5;153mauth.js[39m are pure ESM with no DOM — but nothing exists to run a test with. Setting this up is cheapest right now,
  before the module split lands, and it's the part most likely to be skipped and never revisited.

  My suggestion: [1munit tests via [38;5;153mnode --test[0m (built into Node, zero dependencies, runs [38;5;153m.mjs[39m ESM directly — it fits a repo
  that has deliberately stayed dependency-free) covering the session store's round-trip, corrupt-data recovery,
  unavailable-storage fallback, and that [38;5;153mlogin()[39m persists. I'd skip end-to-end and fuzz/mutation testing entirely — one
  form and a stub don't justify a browser harness. Linting and formatting I'd take or leave; [38;5;153mbiome[39m is a single
  dependency that does both, but this is a six-file repo.

